# Detached dispatch: the fleet supervises its own runs, not the chat harness. 2026-09-13
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/dev-agents.git wave-plans/dev-agents/2026-09-13-detached-dispatch.plan --auto --retries 1
# FIRE AFTER PR 75 (per-seat worktrees) IS MERGED; both touch dispatch.sh.
#
# Why: on 2026-09-13 the chat harness killed six background dispatches on a
# 32 GB machine at 44 percent free memory, because it stops any background
# task it tracks when the system looks low on memory. A dispatch must not be a
# child of the chat session at all.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | devops | Add a detached mode to the dispatcher, branch feat/detached-dispatch. READ FIRST scripts/dispatch.sh (the lock, the queue integration, fleet events, notify.sh), scripts/fleet-events.sh, scripts/queue.sh, docs/local-pr-sentinel.md and the launchd plists already in docs (the sentinel and the worktree sweep) for the house pattern. DELIVER: ONE, scripts/dispatch.sh gains --detach: it re-executes itself under setsid and nohup with stdin closed, all output to logs/dispatch-runs/<dispatch id>.log, writes a pid file next to it, prints the dispatch id and the log path, and returns immediately; the child is a session leader so no parent process, shell or chat session can take it down when that parent exits or is killed; the lock, the queue marks, the fleet events and the notify hooks all behave exactly as in the attached mode. TWO, scripts/dispatch-status.sh <dispatch id> prints running or the final status, the seat table, and the last ten lines of the log, reading the events stream and the pid file; exit 0 when finished, 3 when still running, so a caller can poll it. THREE, scripts/dispatch-wait.sh <dispatch id> [timeout seconds] blocks until the run ends or the timeout, polling every 30 seconds, and prints the same summary; this is the command a chat session calls instead of holding the dispatch as its own background task. FOUR, a launchd user agent, installed the same way as the sentinel and the sweep (docs plist, install and uninstall scripts, make targets), that runs scripts/queue-runner.sh every minute: if no dispatch is running for a repo and the queue has a queued plan for that repo whose blocked reason is empty, it starts that plan with --detach, at most one new start per minute, never more than one running per repo unless per-branch locks allow it, and it writes what it started to the same log directory; off by default until installed, and a single env var pauses it. FIVE, README section: how to run detached, how to check, how the runner works, and the memory note (the harness kill is the reason). VERIFY: bash -n on every touched script; a proof in a tmp clone with the stubbed run-remote: start a dispatch with --detach from a shell, kill that shell process group, show the dispatch still running and finishing with its events and queue marks intact; dispatch-status returns 3 then 0; dispatch-wait returns after the end; the runner starts a queued plan and does not start a second one while the first runs; real exit codes pasted. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a PR. Do not merge. | feat/detached-dispatch

2 | plan-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of branch feat/detached-dispatch in dev-agents. No commits, no push, no edits; tmp writes only. Record the PR head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can the detached run be taken down by its starter, and can the runner ever start two things it should not. Prove: start with --detach, kill the starting shell with SIGKILL on its process group, and show the dispatch finishes with the lock released and the queue settled; run the runner with two queued plans for one repo and show it starts one, waits, then starts the next; run it with a queued plan whose blocked reason is set and show it does not start; pause it with the env var and show nothing starts; confirm dispatch-status exit codes and that dispatch-wait honours the timeout. Check the plist with plutil, the install and uninstall symmetry, and that nothing in the log directory carries a prompt or task body. Post ONE comment on the PR whose first line reads CRITIC DETACHED DISPATCH with SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX and line citations. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/detached-dispatch
